    "problem_statement": "The kernel memory slab API provides no way to declare a slab for objects of a given C type with the alignment derived automatically from that type. Callers must manually specify the block size and alignment when using K_MEM_SLAB_DEFINE / K_MEM_SLAB_DEFINE_STATIC, which is error-prone: an alignment that is smaller than the type's alignment requirement produces misaligned blocks (undefined behaviour, e.g. for a 64-bit member on a 64-bit target). There is an analogous gap in the sys_mem_blocks API via SYS_MEM_BLOCKS_DEFINE / SYS_MEM_BLOCKS_DEFINE_STATIC. The expected behaviour is that helpers exist to declare a slab (public and static) and a mem-blocks object (public and static) from a type, deriving the block size and alignment from that type, and that blocks obtained from such a slab are the expected size, count, and aligned to the type's alignment.",
